-- title:
--   The index set of the discrete quadratic form
-- statement:
--   For a region $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ and a separation threshold $R_0$, the **pair set** is the collection of ordered pairs of lattice points of $S$ that the discrete quadratic form sums over:
--
--   $$P_{S,R_0} \;=\; \big\{(x,y) \ :\ x \in S\cap\mathbb{Z}^d,\ y \in S\cap\mathbb{Z}^d,\ \|x-y\| > R_0\big\}.$$
--
--   Both conditions matter: restricting to the lattice makes the index set countable, and the separation $\|x-y\| > R_0$ is exactly the range in which the discrete kernel bounds are available.
--
--   Naming the index set is what turns the discrete forms on the two balls into sums over explicit finite sets — both balls contain only finitely many lattice points — so that the double counting at the heart of the chaining argument can be carried out by exchanging the order of a finite double sum, rather than by manipulating an infinite series.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The set consists of *ordered* pairs, so each unordered pair appears twice; this matches the convention in the definition of the discrete form. No hypotheses are imposed on $S$ or $R_0$.
